%% CASE Brightmart loyalty app
Brightmart, a retail chain, is building a customer loyalty app with one Scrum team working in two-week sprints. The fully loaded team cost is **$40,000 per sprint**.

| Data | Value |
|---|---|
| Remaining release backlog | 360 story points |
| Velocity, last four sprints | 28, 32, 30, 30 |
| Remaining budget | $440,000 |

The marketing vice president wants the app launched before the holiday season, which is **10 sprints** away. The release burnup chart shows that the total-scope line has risen by 60 points over the last three sprints, mainly because of new requests from marketing.
@@ PR8 | Agile
Based on the average velocity, how many sprints are needed to complete the remaining release backlog?
A) 12
B) 10
C) 11
D) 13
= A
? Average velocity = (28 + 32 + 30 + 30) / 4 = 30 points. 360 / 30 = 12 sprints.

@@ PR6 | Agile | fill
At the current pace and cost, what is the estimated cost to complete the remaining release backlog? (Answer in dollars.)
= $480,000
? 12 sprints × $40,000 = $480,000, which exceeds the remaining budget of $440,000 by $40,000.

@@ PR3 | Agile
The launch date before the holiday season cannot move. What is the best way to deliver value by that date?
A) The product owner prioritises the most valuable features that fit about 300 points
B) The team increases its velocity to 36 points per sprint by working overtime until launch
C) The launch includes all 360 points, with testing reduced in the last sprints to save time
D) The marketing vice president selects the features, since marketing requested the date
= A
? With a fixed date, scope is the variable: 10 sprints × 30 points ≈ 300 points. The product owner orders the backlog so the most valuable features are delivered first; overtime and reduced testing are unsustainable.

@@ PR9 | Agile
How should the project manager use the burnup chart in the next stakeholder review?
A) Show how the added scope pushes out the forecast and agree how to manage scope
B) Hide the scope line so that stakeholders focus on the work the team has already completed
C) Replace the burnup chart with a burndown chart, which does not show changes in total scope
D) Present only the velocity figures, since the burnup chart is meant for the team's own use
= A
? The burnup chart makes scope growth visible. Transparent discussion of its impact helps stakeholders make informed trade-offs rather than being surprised later.
%% END CASE

@@ PR1 | Hybrid
A manufacturer is implementing an ERP system. Configuration of the modules will benefit from frequent user feedback, while data migration and the cut-over weekend require a precise, rehearsed sequence. Which approach fits best?
A) Hybrid: iterative configuration with a predictive plan for migration and cut-over
B) Predictive for everything, since ERP systems are large and complex installations
C) Adaptive for everything, including an exploratory approach to the cut-over weekend
D) Kanban for everything, with the cut-over handled as an ordinary card on the board
= A
? Combining adaptive delivery for uncertain, feedback-driven work with predictive planning for fixed, high-risk sequences is a classic hybrid pattern.

@@ PR1 | Predictive
While integrating the plans, the project manager notices that the risk management plan assumes a 10% contingency reserve, but the cost management plan includes no contingency. What should the project manager do?
A) Align the plans so that reserves are consistent across the integrated plan
B) Keep both plans as they are, since each was approved by a different expert
C) Remove the contingency from the risk plan so it matches the cost plan
D) Let the sponsor decide later if any identified risk actually occurs
= A
? Integrating the plan means ensuring components are consistent. Conflicting assumptions between subsidiary plans must be resolved before baselining.

@@ PR1 | Agile
At which level of agile planning does the team decide which backlog items it will deliver in the next two weeks?
A) Iteration (sprint) planning
B) Product roadmap planning
C) Portfolio planning
D) Release planning
= A
? Iteration planning selects work for the coming sprint. Roadmaps and release plans cover longer horizons at a higher level.

@@ PR1 | Predictive
What does the change management plan, a component of the project management plan, describe?
A) How change requests are submitted, evaluated, approved and incorporated
B) How the organisation will help users adopt the project's deliverables
C) Which changes the sponsor has already approved for the project
D) How the team will change its working practices after each retrospective
= A
? The change management plan describes the integrated change control process. Supporting user adoption is organisational change management, a different topic.

@@ PR1 | Hybrid
A retail chain will roll out a new point-of-sale system to 200 stores. What delivery plan would best reduce risk?
A) A pilot in a few stores, followed by waves of stores using the lessons learned
B) A single nationwide switch-over on one weekend to minimise the total disruption
C) Letting each store decide when to install the system based on its own priorities
D) Installing the system in the largest stores first, since they generate the most revenue
= A
? A pilot followed by waves allows learning and adjustment before full-scale deployment, reducing operational risk.

@@ PR1 | Predictive
During planning, the team assumes that the city will grant building permits within 30 days. What should the project manager do with this assumption?
A) Record it in the assumption log and assess it as a potential risk
B) Treat it as a fact, since the city usually grants permits on time
C) Add 30 days of buffer to every activity in the project schedule
D) Ask the city to sign a contract guaranteeing the 30-day period
= A
? Assumptions are documented and analysed because they may prove false; an inaccurate assumption is a source of risk.

@@ PR1 | Agile
A stakeholder asks for a high-level view of which product themes will be addressed over the next four quarters. Which artifact should the product owner provide?
A) The product roadmap
B) The sprint backlog
C) The iteration burndown chart
D) The definition of done
= A
? A roadmap shows the product's direction and major themes over time. The sprint backlog and burndown cover a single sprint.

@@ PR1 | Predictive | multi
Which two inputs does the project manager mainly need to develop the integrated project management plan? (Choose two.)
A) The project charter
B) The outputs of the other planning processes, such as subsidiary plans
C) Accepted deliverables
D) The final project report
E) Procurement audit results
= A, B
? The integrated plan builds on the charter and the subsidiary plans and baselines from other planning processes. Accepted deliverables, final reports and audit results come later.

@@ PR2 | Agile
The product owner asks why the team wants to build a story map for the new booking service. What is the main purpose of a story map?
A) To arrange stories along the user journey and slice them into releases
B) To assign each user story to the developer who will implement it
C) To record the dependencies between the project's work packages
D) To calculate the story points that the team can complete per sprint
= A
? Story maps show the user journey horizontally and priorities vertically, helping identify a minimum viable release and later slices.

@@ PR2 | Predictive
The sales and logistics departments have submitted 140 requirements, many of them conflicting. The project cannot deliver all of them in the first phase. What should the project manager do?
A) Facilitate a prioritisation workshop with both departments, using MoSCoW
B) Accept the requirements of the department with the largest budget for the next financial year
C) Include all 140 requirements and extend the schedule so that every department is satisfied
D) Let the development team choose the requirements that are easiest to implement first
= A
? A facilitated workshop with an agreed prioritisation technique reconciles conflicting needs transparently. The other options ignore value or create conflict.

@@ PR2 | Hybrid
Which statement best describes project scope as opposed to product scope?
A) The work needed to deliver the product with its specified features
B) The features and functions that characterise the product, service or result
C) The business benefits that the organisation expects to realise from the product
D) The list of requirements documented in the requirements traceability matrix
= A
? Project scope is the work; product scope is the set of features and functions. Benefits and requirements are related but different concepts.

@@ PR2 | Predictive
While reviewing the WBS, the project manager finds a work package that cannot be traced to any approved requirement. What should the project manager do?
A) Check its origin and remove it unless it is formally confirmed as needed
B) Keep it, since additional work usually adds value for the customer
C) Ask the team to complete it quickly before the customer notices
D) Move it into the contingency reserve as unplanned work
= A
? Work that cannot be traced to a requirement may be gold plating. The RTM helps check; unjustified work should be removed or formally approved.

@@ PR2 | Agile
Stories regularly enter sprints without clear acceptance criteria, causing rework and disputes at the sprint review. What should the team agree on?
A) A definition of ready requiring acceptance criteria before the sprint
B) A longer sprint review in which the acceptance criteria are written afterwards
C) A rule that the scrum master writes acceptance criteria during the sprint
D) A practice of accepting all stories that the developers consider complete
= A
? A definition of ready ensures backlog items are clear enough to be worked on, including acceptance criteria agreed with the product owner.

@@ PR2 | Predictive
During validation, the customer accepts a deliverable but notes two minor cosmetic defects to be fixed. What should the project manager do?
A) Record the conditional acceptance and schedule the defect repairs
B) Refuse the acceptance until the two defects have been corrected
C) Ignore the defects, since the customer has accepted the deliverable
D) Submit a change request to remove cosmetic quality from the scope
= A
? Acceptance with minor findings is documented, and the defects are repaired as agreed. Ignoring them damages trust; refusing acceptance is unnecessary.

@@ PR2 | Hybrid
Customers are delighted by a new "one-click reorder" feature, although none of them asked for it. In the Kano model, how is this feature classified today?
A) An excitement (delighter) feature
B) A basic (must-be) feature
C) A performance (linear) feature
D) An indifferent feature
= A
? Unexpected features that create delight are excitement features. Over time they tend to become expected performance or basic features.

@@ PR2 | Predictive | match
Match each requirement with its type.
1) "Increase online sales by 15% within a year"
2) "Store managers need a daily stock report"
3) "The system shall calculate delivery charges by postcode"
4) "The system shall be available 99.9% of the time"
A) Business requirement
B) Stakeholder requirement
C) Functional requirement
D) Non-functional requirement
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? Business requirements express organisational goals; stakeholder requirements express a group's needs; functional requirements describe behaviour; non-functional requirements describe qualities such as availability.

@@ PR3 | Agile
Feature X will bring $50,000 per week once live and takes two weeks to build. Feature Y will bring $20,000 per week and takes one week. Using cost of delay divided by duration, which feature should be built first?
A) Feature X, with a score of 25 versus 20
B) Feature Y, because it is quicker to build
C) Feature Y, with a score of 20 versus 25
D) Feature X, because it is more expensive
= A
? X: 50,000 / 2 = 25,000 per week of effort; Y: 20,000 / 1 = 20,000. Building X first maximises value delivered over time.

@@ PR3 | Predictive
The project will close next month, but its benefits — lower energy costs — will only be measurable over the next two years. Who should track the benefits after closure?
